{"id": "174020", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=174020", "title": "Shenkeng", "text": "Shenkeng (\u6df1\u5751\u5340 \"Sh\u0113nk\u0113ng\" in Mandarin, \"Chhim-khi\u207f\" in Hokkien) is a district of New Taipei. \nUnderstand.\nThe district used to be the home of the indigenous Pingpu people.\nShenkeng is known in Taiwan for its tofu products and restaurants and indeed tourists coming to the area to sample the tofu make up the major part of the local economy.\nGet in.\nBy road.\nShenkeng is not connected to any of the rail networks in Taiwan and the only way to access the district is by road.\nEat.\nLocal specialties.\nShenkeng has received a significant amount of press for its tofu, particularly in Japan and Hong Kong, making the region popular with tourists from within Taiwan, as well as the wider region."}
{"id": "174021", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=174021", "title": "Shiding", "text": "Shiding (\u77f3\u7887\u5340) is a rural district of New Taipei, with a population of about 7,000. It is the site of the Fetsui Reservoir, which provides much of the water to the cities in the northern part of Taiwan island.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nThe district is accessible by bus from the Taipei metro.\nDrink.\nPouchong (baozhong) tea is a lightly-oxidized (greener) oolong tea, produced in the hills south of Taipei. Its name means \"the wrapped kind\", as it was historically wrapped in paper while drying."}
{"id": "174028", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=174028", "title": "Matap\u00e9dia", "text": "Matap\u00e9dia is a municipality in Matap\u00e9dia Valley area of the Gasp\u00e9 Peninsula in eastern Quebec. It sits on the banks of the Restigouche River along the New Brunswick border.\nUnderstand.\nMatap\u00e9dia is a small village located at the meeting of the Matap\u00e9dia and Ristigouche rivers, both of which are internationally renowned for salmon fishing. In fact, the name of the village comes from the Micmac language and means \"where the rivers meet\".\nHistory.\nMatap\u00e9dia became the first settlement in the Matap\u00e9dia Valley when Loyalists came to settle there following the American Revolution. Subsequently, the area saw the arrival of Acadian, Irish, and French-Canadian settlers, as well as English Canadians from the Maritime provinces. The different cultures still exist in harmony today.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nMatap\u00e9dia is located along Quebec Route 132, which functions as a ring road around the Gasp\u00e9 Peninsula east of Rimouski. The Matap\u00e9dia Bridge cross the Matap\u00e9dia River into New Brunswick and continues as New Brunswick Route 11."}
