{"id": "174266", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=174266", "title": "Homewood (Alabama)", "text": "Homewood is located in Metropolitan Alabama, a suburb of Birmingham famous as the home of Samford University.\nGet in.\nFly in to Birmingham-Shuttlesworth International Airport, then take I-65 south.\nMAX Transit, the public transit authority for Birmingham and Jefferson County, also offers service to downtown Homewood on its Magic City Connector bus.\nSee.\nEven if you're not interested in any of the sights here, it's worth it just to visit downtown Homewood, a scenic enclave on the southern foot of Red Mountain, just minutes from downtown Birmingham."}
{"id": "174267", "revid": "2432955", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=174267", "title": "Zaouiat Ahansal", "text": "Zaouiat Ahansal is in the High Atlas region of Morocco.\nUnderstand.\nA remote High Atlas mountain town, Zaouiat Ahansal was founded in the 13th century by traveller and Islamic scholar Sidi Said Ahansal, who claimed in legend, was intended to establish a religious school where his cat leapt off from his donkey.\nThe town has a population of 10,000 people and is inhabited by nomadic Berber people, most notably the A\u00eft Atta tribes.\nGo next.\nThe oasis town of Tinerhir is close by."}
{"id": "174273", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=174273", "title": "Bessemer", "text": "Bessemer is an industrial city in Metropolitan Alabama, just outside of Birmingham.\nUnderstand.\nBessemer was the site of much of Alabama's 20th-century steel industry, the decline of which, beginning in the 1960s and '70s, has left in its wake a visible degree of economic problems for the local populace. Still, the city is of interest to aficionados of industrial history - and also foodies, being home to both Alabama's oldest operating restaurant and one of its most renowned barbecue joints."}
