{"id": "175429", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=175429", "title": "Al Jawf (Libya)", "text": "Al Jawf is a town of 31,000 people (2009) in Kufra district, southeastern Libya. The city lies in the largest oasis in the Kufra basin.\nUnderstand.\nIt is one of the most heavily irrigated oases in the Sahara. "}
{"id": "175431", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=175431", "title": "Ubari", "text": "Ubari is a Tuareg-speaking oasis town and the capital of the Wadi al Hayaa District in Libya.\nUnderstand.\nIt is in the Idehan Ubari, a Libyan section of the Sahara Desert. Ubari is in the Targa valley, lying between the Messak Sattafat plateau and Idhan Ubari erg sand dunes and lakes. Native plants include wetland grasses at the natural spring fed lakes' shorelines, and the native Saharan Date palm (Phoenix dactylifera)\nThe Ubari oasis settlement is the second center for the Kel Ajjer Tuareg people, after Ghat. Neighbouring villages include Germa, and Garran.\nClimate.\nUbari is in one of the sunniest and driest areas in the world. It has short, very warm winters but long, extremely hot summers. Average annual rainfall is one of the lowest found on the planet with only 8 mm (0.31 in) and many decades may easily pass without seeing any rainfall at all. Ubari has permanent, unlimited sunshine and clear skies all year-round and in all seasons. Clouds are extremely rare over this bone-dry land. Average high temperatures exceed 40 \u00b0C (104 \u00b0F) from June to September."}
{"id": "175437", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=175437", "title": "Akhalkalaki", "text": "Akhalkalaki (Georgian: \u10d0\u10ee\u10d0\u10da\u10e5\u10d0\u10da\u10d0\u10e5\u10d8, Armenian: \u0531\u056d\u0561\u056c\u0584\u0561\u056c\u0561\u0584) is a town in the Samtskhe-Javakheti region of Georgia.\nUnderstand.\nThe transit city is located about 30 km from the Armenian border and 90\u202f% of its 7500 people (2024) are ethnic Armenians. \nGet in.\nBy marshrutka.\nThe return journeys should be equally frequent.\nBy train.\nAkhalkalaki lies close to the Kars-Tbilisi route, with just one train per week at the beginning of this connection.\nGo next.\n... or try straight to Bakuriani."}
{"id": "175438", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=175438", "title": "Tskaltubo", "text": "Tskaltubo (also \"Tsqaltubo\") is a town in the Rioni Region of Georgia. Tskaltubo is a great place to remember the great era of the 20th century when it was a mineral spa with many sanatoria and grand hotels. You can explore the old buildings and get a feeling for the now gone time.\nUnderstand.\nTskaltubo's architecture is a synthesis of Stalinist period classical style and of Georgian ethnic decor with Gothic and Roman features.\nThe \"Spring No. 6\", is the largest thermal bath working today. It was built in 1950 exclusively for Joseph Stalin, leader of the Soviet Union at the time. Private baths and dachas of Stalin and Lavrenti Beria are still kept in the city. The mineral water flows directly into Buildings #1 and #6. There are five pools of mineral water, 37 individual cabins for bathing mineral water and 17 hydro-massage cabinets.\nIn 2024, the town had a population of 7,400 people.\nGet in.\nMarshrutkas 4, 30, 34 and 44 go regularly between Tskaltubo and Kutaisi, taking 20 minutes and two lari\u2014try the western side of the Red Bridge in Kutaisi if you cannot determine a different starting point.\nGet around.\nSmall enough to explore on foot.\nSee.\nCaves:\nFurther afield:"}
