{"id": "175451", "revid": "2431743", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=175451", "title": "Osku", "text": "Osku is a city in northwestern Iran. It is the capital of Osku County, in East Azerbaijan Province, Iran. Osku is known as the capital of batik in Iran. As of 2016, its population was about 19,000.\nUnderstand.\nOsku is one of the oldest cities in East Azerbaijan according to old books and is like stepping into a time machine. The city is located on a hillside volcano and has many caves in the city. The inhabitants of Osku are Azerbaijan Turks, who have lived in the city for more than 100 years. The tourist attractions such as\u00a0Kandovan, Osku and natural landscapes etc make it pleasant enough for tourism and worth seeing amongst destinations in Iran. It is one of the few cities in Iran where batik printing centers still remain in operation."}
{"id": "175457", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=175457", "title": "Warm Springs (Virginia)", "text": "Warm Springs is the county seat of Bath County in Shenandoah Valley region of Virginia. The area also include the settlement of Hot Springs."}
{"id": "175465", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=175465", "title": "Santiago del Teide", "text": "Santiago del Teide is a town of 11,000 people (2019) on the west coast of Tenerife.\nUnderstand.\nThe town is right in the middle among all hiking and tourist destinations in the area, even though Los Gigantes is the more convenient hub with numerous accommodation options.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe municipality is well-connected to the south by the TF-1, which in the future will continue northwards from here through a tunnel under the Teno massif. As of early 2024, however, crossing the mountains still means curvy mountain roads. From Icod de los Vinos, you can take the nice and straight TF-5 to El Tanque from where the very curvy TF-82 will take you over the mountains. The TF-373 is a more quiet and less windy alternative, but it takes you further away from civilization before merging into the TF-82 a bit north of Santiago del Teide. Other access roads are the TF-436 from Buenavista del Norte via Masca and the TF-454 and TF-82 from Los Gigantes via Tamaimo.\nGet around.\nTaxis are abundant in case you miss the bus to Masca.\nOtherwise, the town is very walkable.\nDo.\nHiking.\nBesides the opportunities below, also check out Los Gigantes#Hiking and Teno Rural Park#Hiking, which are just around the corner, and with Los Gigantes probably being your preferred place of stay overnight anyway.\nBasically there are so many hiking trails northeast of Santiago that you don't really need to bother about which one you take, as long as you have a destination. The whole area is also called, where you can roam around as you please.\nBuy.\nThe town is overly touristy, you will thus probably find enough to spend your money on.\nSleep.\nYou will find most accommodation options in Los Gigantes, Puerto de Santiago, or Playa de la Arena. Otherwise, check out \"San Jos\u00e9 de los LLanos\":\nCamping.\nThe closest official camp grounds nearby are:"}
{"id": "175469", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=175469", "title": "G\u00f6yg\u00f6l", "text": "G\u00f6yg\u00f6l is a town just south of Ganja in Ganja Region of Azerbaijan.\nUnderstand.\nG\u00f6yg\u00f6l is famous for its German founders that came here in the hope of a better life. Formerly named \"Khanlar\" (Xanlar) and \"Helenendorf\", it was founded by German farmers from Bavarian Swabia. Today, the town remains an old German neighbourhood in excellent conditions. A local museum shows the finds (e.g. bronze weapons, jewels, pottery, etc.) extracted from a large cemetery excavated in 1990.\nGet around.\nWalking is the preferred method, otherwise you will miss all the interesting sights.\nHowever, there are 1-2 local buses running between the hospital and the bus station."}
{"id": "175476", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=175476", "title": "Armamar", "text": "Armamar is a community of 6,300 people (2011) in Alto Douro wine region of Tr\u00e1s-os-Montes e Alto Douro, northern Portugal. Armamar's economy thrives on the production of mountain apples, Douro and Porto wines, cheeses, smoked meats and sausages. The apple is the product of greatest economic importance and that is why Armamar is known as \"Capital of the Mountain Apple\".\nUnderstand.\nArmamar (uhr-muh-MAHR, /\u0250\u027e.m\u0250.\u02c8ma\u027e/) municipality has 6,297 residents with about 1,400 living in the city proper.\nGet in.\nArmamar can be reached via the A24 motorway (\"Autoestrada do Interior Norte\") to the EN313 road."}
{"id": "175488", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=175488", "title": "Kotkapura", "text": "Kotkapura is a city of 92,000 people (2011) in Punjab (India). It is the largest city in the Faridkot District. Kot Kapura has also been called the 'City of White Gold' due to its cotton market, which was once the largest cotton market in Asia. \nUnderstand.\nThe city is known for its \"Dhodha Sweet\" and \"Atta Chicken\" both of which are exported outside India.\nClimate.\nThe climate of the Kotkapura is, on the whole, dry and is characterized by a very hot summer, a short rainy season, and a cold winters. The year may be divided into four seasons. The cold season is from November to March. This is followed by the summer season which lasts until about the end of June. The period from July to mid-September constitutes the south-westerly monsoon season. The latter half of September and October may be termed the post-monsoon or the transition period.\nGet in.\nIt takes around 15 minutes by bus from Faridkot, 4 hours by road from Chandigarh, 2 hours from Ludhiana, and 8 hours from New Delhi by train to reach the city. It is a central city on route to Ganganagar, Ludhiana, Bathinda, Firozpur. \nBy plane.\nSri Guru Ram Dass Jee International Airport in Amritsar and Bathinda domestic airport are the nearest, about 90 km away.\nBuy.\nAn agricultural centre in the Faridkot district of Punjab, Kotkapura is known for its large cotton market."}
{"id": "175499", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=175499", "title": "Tabua\u00e7o", "text": "Tabua\u00e7o is a village of 6,350 people (2011) in the Alto Douro wine region of Tr\u00e1s-os-Montes e Alto Douro, northern Portugal.\nUnderstand.\nThe roots of the village of Tabua\u00e7o (tuh-boo-AH-soo, /t\u0250.bu.\u02c8a.su/) date back to times older than Portuguese nationality. During prehistory, several peoples settled here leaving the ruins of shelters and castros, and the remains of work instruments, such as ceramic pieces or primitive oil presses and wine and oil presses.\nAgriculture remains the most important sector in the region, employing over half of the active population. Wine and handicrafts are among the products of greatest economic importance.\nGet in.\nThe nearest motorway is the A24 (\"Autoestrada do Interior Norte\"). Northbound take exit 10 to N313; southbound take exit 11 to N2. Then take N222 to EM512. \nIntercity bus service is available from Rede Expressos[dead link]."}
{"id": "175528", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=175528", "title": "Guigang", "text": "Guigang (\u8d35\u6e2f) is a prefecture city in Central Guangxi.\nUnderstand.\nGuigang is not a tourist city and is unlikely to be on anybody's bucket list. That being said, it does have a few things going for it. For instance, the city is relatively uncrowded by Chinese standards and there are a number of decent parks. If you get bored, Guiping, the nearest moderately touristy city, is only 20 minutes away by high-speed train.\nGet around.\nBuses are available to all areas of the city. One rather useful bus is the no. 8 bus, which operates between the Guigang Museum and Nanshan Park, and also stops at East Lake Park and a couple of other parks."}
{"id": "175530", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=175530", "title": "S\u00e3o Jo\u00e3o da Pesqueira", "text": "S\u00e3o Jo\u00e3o da Pesqueira is a town of 7,900 people (2011) in the Alto Douro wine region of Tr\u00e1s-os-Montes e Alto Douro, northern Portugal.\nUnderstand.\nS\u00e3o Jo\u00e3o da Pesqueira (SUHNGOO zhoo-UHNGOO duh p(ih)sh-KAY-ruh, /\u02c8s\u0250\u0303w \u0292u.\u02c8\u0250\u0303w d\u0250 p\u0268\u0283.\u02c8k\u0250j.\u027e\u0250/) is the oldest recognized municipality in the country, created in 1055, thus taking precedence over such major cities as Coimbra, Guimar\u00e3es or Lamego.\nSituated in the heart of the Douro wine region, S\u00e3o Jo\u00e3o da Pesqueira is well endowed with vineyards that support the production of vintage and port wines.\nGet in.\nThe nearest motorways are A4 (\"Autoestrada Transmontana\" or \"Autoestrada de Tr\u00e1s-os-Montes e Alto Douro\") and A24 (\"Autoestrada do Interior Norte\").\nDo.\nThere is a network of waymarked hiking trails, including a section of the GR 14 Porto to Strasbourg Rota dos Vinhos da Europa (European Wine Route trail)."}
{"id": "175532", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=175532", "title": "Carrazeda de Ansi\u00e3es", "text": "Carrazeda de Ansi\u00e3es is a town of 6,400 people (2011) in Alto Douro wine region of Tr\u00e1s-os-Montes e Alto Douro, northern Portugal.\nUnderstand.\nCarrazeda de Ansi\u00e3es (kuh-rruh-ZIH-duh d(ih) uhn-see-UHNEESH, /ka.\u0281\u0250.\u02c8ze.d\u0250 d\u0268 \u0250\u0303.si.\u02c8\u0250\u0303j\u0283/) municipality has 6,373 residents (2011), with about 1,700 in the town itself.\nGet in.\nThe nearest motorway is the A4 (\"Autoestrada de Tr\u00e1s-os-Montes e Alto Douro\"), from which the IC5 road provides access to the town.\nSee.\nMuch of the town's architecture dates to the late 19th century, and it has some medieval structures that highlight the region's history. \nThe following are classified as National Monuments (Monumentos Nacionais) in the municipality of Carrazeda de Ansi\u00e3es:"}
{"id": "175534", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=175534", "title": "Moimenta da Beira", "text": "Moimenta da Beira is a town of 10,200 people (2010) in Beira Alta region of Portugal.\nUnderstand.\nMoimenta da Beira (mawy-MEHN-tuh duh BAY-ruh, /m\u0254j.\u02c8m\u1ebd.t\u0250 d\u0250 \u02c8b\u0250j.\u027e\u0250/) is famous for the production of wine, apple, mainly of the \"bravo de esmolfe\" species, and also for having been the municipality where Aquilino Ribeiro lived in various stages of his life, more precisely in the small village of Soutosa.\nGet in.\nBus service is available from Rede Expressos[dead link].\nThe nearest motorway is A24 (\"Autoestrada do Interior Norte\"), which connects to town via the EN226 road."}
{"id": "175544", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=175544", "title": "Biatorb\u00e1gy", "text": "Biatorb\u00e1gy (German: \"Wiehall-Kleinturwall\") is a town in Pest County. The inner area of the town is in the Zs\u00e1mb\u00e9k Basin, it is surrounded by the Buda Hills, its eastern part extends into the Buda\u00f6rs Basin.\nUnderstand.\nBiatorb\u00e1gy was formed by the union of the villages of Torb\u00e1gy in the north and Bia in the south in 1966. The villages were first mentioned in 1192 as \"Biua\" and \"Torb\u00e1gyerd\u0151\". Biatorb\u00e1gy was elevated to town status in 2007.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nTrains \u00a0S10\u00a0 and \u00a0S12\u00a0 are available hourly from Budapest-D\u00e9li p\u00e1lyaudvar to Gy\u0151r via Bicske, Tatab\u00e1nya, Tata and Kom\u00e1rom. The journey from Budapest takes 21 minutes and costs.\nBy bus.\nFrom Budapest to Biatorb\u00e1gy: regional buses from Kelenf\u00f6ld vas\u00fat\u00e1llom\u00e1s\u00a0\u00a0M4\u00a0 are available hourly. The journey takes 32 minutes and costs.\nRegional bus line network map of Southwest Pest County.\nGet around.\nBy bus.\nHomm operates local buses in Biatorb\u00e1gy. Daily ticket (in 2020):. Monthly pass (in 2020):. Tickets cannot be purchased at the driver, you have to buy them online before the journey.\nSee.\nFurther afield.\nIn P\u00e1ty:\nConnect.\nBiatorb\u00e1gy's postal code is H-2051, and its telephone area code is 23."}
{"id": "175549", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=175549", "title": "Sernancelhe", "text": "Sernancelhe is a town and municipality in Beira Alta, Portugal.\nUnderstand.\nSernancelhe (s(ih)r-nuhn-SEH-ly(ih), /s\u0268\u027e.n\u0250\u0303.\u02c8se.\u028e\u0268/) municipality has 5,713 residents (2021 preliminary), with about 1,100 living in town.\nGet in.\nIntercity bus service is provided by Rede Expressos[dead link].\nThe N226 road provides access from Lamego, Celorico da Beira, and from the A24 motorway (\"Autoestrada do Interior Norte\"). The N229 connects from Viseu, S\u00e3o Jo\u00e3o da Pesqueira, and the IP5 main route."}
{"id": "175550", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=175550", "title": "Freixo de Espada \u00e0 Cinta", "text": "Freixo de Espada \u00e0 Cinta is a town of 3,800 people (2011) in Alto Douro wine region of Tr\u00e1s-os-Montes e Alto Douro, northern Portugal.\nUnderstand.\nFreixo de Espada \u00e0 Cinta (FRAY-shoo d(ih) sh-PAH-duh AH SEEN-tuh, /\u02c8f\u027e\u0250j.\u0283u d\u0268 \u0283.\u02c8pa.d\u0250 \u02c8a \u02c8s\u0129.t\u0250/) is 180 km northeast of Porto, 400 km northeast of Lisbon, and 100 km south of Bragan\u00e7a. It is bordered on the north by the municipality of Mogadouro, to the west by Torre de Moncorvo, east by the province of Salamanca in Spain, and south by the municipality of Figueira de Castelo Rodrigo.\nGet in.\nRede Expressos[dead link] provides long-distance bus service to Freixo.\nFreixo can be reached via the EN220, EN221, and EN325 roads."}
{"id": "175551", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=175551", "title": "Vila Nova de Foz C\u00f4a", "text": "Vila Nova de Foz C\u00f4a is a town of 7,300 people (2011) in Alto Douro wine region of Tr\u00e1s-os-Montes e Alto Douro, northern Portugal. It principal draw for visitors are the sites of prehistoric rock art scattered through the nearby C\u00f4a Valley (a UNESCO World Heritage Site), and the museum in town that serves as a portal for understanding them.\nUnderstand.\nVila Nova de Foz C\u00f4a (VEE-luh NAW-vuh d(ih) FAWSH KOH-uh, /vi.\u026b\u0250 \u02c8n\u0254.v\u0250 d\u0268 \u02c8f\u0254\u0283 \u02c8ko.\u0250/)\nThe rock engravings were discovered in 1994. They are supported by vertical shale surfaces, with preferential exposure to the east. The dimensions of the engravings vary between 15 cm and 180 cm, although those 40\u201350 cm in length predominate. The engraving techniques used are perforation and abrasion, which sometimes coexist, with the abrasion regularizing the perforation. The strokes are wide, although they are sometimes accompanied by a large amount of fine strokes, which served as a sketch or complemented the previous ones. In other cases, these thin lines draw shapes that are difficult to perceive. There are also engravings filled with multiple strokes.\nGet in.\nAccess to Vila Nova de Foz C\u00f4a from the south is via the EN 102 (which passes through Trancoso and Marialva), and from the north via IP 2 (which connects the town to Bragan\u00e7a and Pocinho).\nThere is no train service.\nEat.\nFresh vegetables and tasty fruits give the menus the natural flavour of the products. Its white and red wines are full-bodied or strong, like all genuine Douro wines. The local bread has a pleasant flavour, of wheat or rye, goes well with cheese, chorizo \u200b\u200bor olives.\nThe oil is also characteristic of the region and can keep cooked succulent saut\u00e9ed with tender sprouts, cabbage and sprouts. The fish in the river Douro and its tributaries, pork, lamb or goat and game such as rabbit, hare and partridge are appreciated dishes.\nThe fruit is varied in late summer, fleshy peaches, honey figs, delicious melons, oranges and grapes. However, it is the dried fruits, especially the almond, that provide the raw material for the most exquisite culinary specialties: the almond sweets, the supplications, the egg lampreys and also the \"coscor\u00f5es\", the folares and the rough balls, freed and stings. "}
